   "text": "# The Printing Press: A History of the Machine That Remade the World\n\nFew inventions in human history have exerted as profound and far-reaching an influence as the printing press. Before its emergence in fifteenth-century Europe, the written word was the preserve of a small elite\u2014scribes, scholars, clerics, and the wealthy\u2014who could afford the enormous expense and time required to produce a single manuscript. The printing press changed all of that. It democratized knowledge, accelerated the spread of ideas, fueled revolutions both religious and political, standardized languages, and laid the groundwork for the scientific revolution, the Enlightenment, and the modern information age. To trace the history of the printing press is, in many ways, to trace the history of modernity itself. This essay will examine the long prehistory of printing, the revolutionary innovations of Johannes Gutenberg, the rapid spread of the technology across Europe, its transformative cultural and political consequences, and its continuing evolution into the present day.\n\n## Precursors: The Long Road to Movable Type\n\nThe story of the printing press does not begin in 1450s Germany but rather many centuries earlier and thousands of miles to the east. The impulse to reproduce texts mechanically is ancient. The Romans, for instance, operated a thriving book trade in which",
